Z||000000||MrPBody||11-10-2001||11:04 PM||||Based on <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/015204.html" TARGET=_blank>recent experience</A>, here are a few new products Rodney might consider:<P>&gt; Adjustable pushrod for clutch master cylinder, stronger than the one I made.<P>&gt; An automatic filler for the clutch master cylinder reservoir.  I envision a larger-volume reservoir that screws-on in place of the reservoir cap.<P>&gt; A jacking block (plastic or aluminum?) to allow use of a floor jack at the rocker panel jacking point.<P>No charge for the ideas; consider it a service to the Fiero community.  <I>&lt;smiles modestly&gt;</I><BR>||||reg||3
Z||000001||Rodney||11-11-2001||09:04 AM||||Based on recent experience, here are a few new products Rodney might consider:<BR>&gt; Adjustable pushrod for clutch master cylinder, stronger than the one I made.<P>==========================================<P>Doable. Is it needed?????  Would it be worthwhile???||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadero2dmax||11-11-2001||09:18 AM||||I used a Wilwood brake Master Cylinder for the clutch on my Q4. Besides having a larger reservoir capacity, it had a bleeder right on the MC, which made bleeding the clutch a whole lot easier. :-)<P>------------------<BR>G<BR>Daily Driver - <BR>85 SE<BR>4.9 Cad<BR>Isuzu 5 speed<BR>'88 cradle<BR>lowered 2 1/2"<BR>Mr. Mikes interior<P>GRM $2002 Challenge Fiero<BR>86 coupe<BR>4.9 (carburated)<BR>2.86:1 TH-125 w/shift kit||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPBody||11-11-2001||11:51 AM||||Rodney --<P><I>I</I> sure needed one!  And mine does work.<P>The pushrod that came with my new master cylinder was too short, although the master cylinder appears in every other respect identical to my old one -- both first-design aluminum-body, made by Girling.  How often does this happen?  I don't know; however, I have read a few other posts by people who solved their clutch problems by extending the pushrod.  Maybe they also bought funny cylinders, or maybe they're compensating for some other problem.<P>I also wonder how many variations there are in design length and tip configuration.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||11-11-2001||08:55 PM||||Part number?  Inner Diameter? (same as our stock clutch MC?)<P>Work well?  Very Well?<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadero2dmax:<BR><B>I used a Wilwood brake Master Cylinder for the clutch on my Q4. Besides having a larger reservoir capacity, it had a bleeder right on the MC, which made bleeding the clutch a whole lot easier. :-)<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||cadero2dmax||11-12-2001||12:03 PM||||Well, let me start at the beginning. <P>In the Q4 racecar, I installed a Rocketparts Racing Quad 4 motor, and the 3.94:1 ratio 5 speed Getrag with an internal Thorsen-Gleason d\limited slip differential. <P>This transmission was out of the later model Getrags with an internal slave cylinder. This in itself posed some "engineering" problems, but after I got it all together, the pedal throw was still OK, but almost to the floor before disengage and just subtle movement to engage. This is not an ideal setup for any type of racing. I needed a larger capacity master cylinder - no way to get around it.<P>I looked at the Blazer cylinder, but for about $75 (if memory serves me) the standard Wilwood brake kit came to mind. I had one from my involvement with Legends, so I modified the mounting, installed the proper size rod end, and my problem was solved. <P>I believe it was the 7/8" single piston, single cylinder kit. The kit had a couple reservoirs, and different mounting options. Photos are in my Photopoint page, you will have to look for the two photos of this part of the conversion. <BR> <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1044072" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1044072</A>  <P>Hope this helps<P>G<P>G||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||11-12-2001||01:18 PM||||I think that the jacking block would be a very good idea.  I used to have a piece of lead that I would use with my floor jack at the rocker jacking point, but one day I lost it becuase (I think) I was in a hurry to get my car back on the road, and the lead usually sticks to the rocker panel from all the wieght on it.  Since i was in a hurry, I must have forgotten to pry it off, and I went for a drive, and have never seen it since.<P>I think that that jacking block would be a wise investment.<P>Another item, that will definatly have a very high demand, is the remanufacture of the steel clutch pedal.  They are a PITA to have, and they are no longer available from GM or elsewhere.  How hard would this be to make?  I am sure that you could make it profitable, Rodney.||||reg||1
